  "review": {
    "verdict": "keep",
    "basis": "Reasoning-only review of the supplied source and Codex evidence. I ran no tests or benchmarks and did not verify the SHA-256.",
    "correctness_review": {
      "cursor_reuse": "Sound. `cursor` is last used for CSR in the neighbor-fill loop of `solve()`; `augment` reads only `offsets`/`neighbors`. The reached list gets the root once, then only a `v` with `!outer[v] && parent[v] == -1`, whose parent is set immediately so it cannot be appended again. Its mate cannot already be listed: had the mate been reached as inner, `v` would already be outer; afterwards the mate is outer and takes the blossom branch. `mark` only sets parents of already-listed vertices. So each vertex appears at most once, `reached <= n`, `tail <= n`, with no overflow.",
      "reached_only_blossom_update": "Equivalent to the full 0..n-1 scan. `contracted` is stamped only on bases of path vertices, which are all reached, and an unreached vertex has `base[i] == i`, so it can never match the stamp.",
      "generation_wrap": "Sound. On uint8 wrap both `ancestor` and `contracted` are zeroed and the generation restarts at 1, so stamp 0 is never live. `join` stamps before `mark` and the rescan use the same generation, and the persistent stamps across `augment` calls stay valid without a per-call clear.",
      "search_core": "`join`, `mark`, the skip condition (`base[u] == base[v] || mate[u] == v`) and the path flip match the standard iterative Edmonds blossom BFS. Using `outer[v]` as the blossom test is equivalent to the root-or-`parent[mate[v]] != -1` form, because contracted inner vertices are set outer. Trying each root once is valid: a root whose search fails stays unaugmentable after later augmentations. No recursion.",
      "upper_bound": "Valid. Every matched vertex has degree at least 1, since matched pairs only arise along edges and `clear_edges` clears the matching. `active_vertices` is recomputed from the full edge list on every non-trivial `solve()`, so `floor(active/2)` holds after incremental additions; reaching it proves maximality. Zero-degree roots are correctly skipped.",
      "n_half_early_return": "Sound. `cardinality == floor(n/2)` is trivially maximum; it only sets `maximum_` and does not touch `partners_`. `is_perfect()` still tests `2 * cardinality == n`, so odd n stays non-perfect and the empty graph stays perfect.",
      "exception_safety_raii": "The workspace (all vectors) is constructed before any write to `partners_` or `cardinality_`; CSR building touches only the workspace; `augment` is noexcept and allocation-free. The `n + 1` and `2m` guards precede allocation. Ownership is vector-only; copy-and-swap and the move operations are correct.",
      "main_io": "Sentinel integer parsing is safe: `peek()` guarantees `position_ < size_` on loop entry, `buffer_[size_] = 0` stops the digit scan, and a refill happens only when the scan ends exactly at `size_`. `to_chars` has 22 bytes of headroom after a flush check. Output is flushed by the static destructor. `reserve_edges(m)` is harmless for valid input. The GCC O3 pragma precedes all function definitions and is guarded off for Clang.",
      "issues_found": "None affecting correctness. Cosmetic only: the header's Submission URL points at the earlier submission and should be updated after resubmission."
    },
    "performance_review": {
      "kernel": "The roughly 21% (GCC) and 20.9% (Clang) kernel reductions are plausible from the design: blossom rescans are O(reached) instead of O(n), mark clears become a stamp increment, and the active-vertex bound ends the root loop early.",
      "whole_program": "The 2.17% wall and 3.39% CPU gains are dominated by process startup and I/O and are near noise for small cases. Only the dense-case gain (about 15% on max_random) is meaningful there.",
      "design_choice": "Preferring the existing byte generation over 32-bit shared stamps is reasonable at this problem scale: it keeps the footprint small, and a wrap costs O(n) once per 255 contractions.",
      "online": "No online time can be inferred. The earlier 9 ms AC is history, and the new code must be resubmitted to confirm AC and timing."
    },
    "further_candidate": null,
    "further_candidate_note": "The one remaining idea is replacing the per-augment O(n) fills of `parent`/`base`/`outer` with a reset over the reached list. It is UNMEASURED and I do not recommend it: at Library Checker scale the fills are small vectorised memsets, the kernel is no longer the whole-program bottleneck, and the change adds risk on the early-return path."
  },
